Over the 1,680 returning requests all five arms sent before minute 110 (HiCache, Old oracle, JIT p50, JIT exact, All four rules):
mean TTFT 286.05, 185.54, 123.09, 130.80 and 113.61 s. Requests served in 2 h: 2,090, 2,611, 3,683, 3,598 and 4,016; users admitted:
303, 346, 459, 450 and 484 of the 729 that arrive.
Recorded waits at x1, one run per arm. 0.1 users arrive a second and at most 128 are in session; the others wait for a slot, a wait
TTFT leaves out. Prompts are synthetic token ids of the recorded lengths and outputs have the recorded length; sessions with a
request over 65,536 tokens are left out. Exact hints are the true next-request times; p50 hints are the median wait after the tool
call the reply makes, from a table the client keeps online for each harness and model. The old oracle asks for a request's evicted
history once its admission is expected within 120 s, and at once when the request is already queued; just-in-time starts the read so
that it lands as the request is admitted. The rules arm gets that table's p10, p50 and p90 and an end-of-session signal: it starts
reads by expected cost at a learned price on host memory, evicts by chance of return, and lets an arrived request's read take room
from early reads.
